{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the archive read settlement head 330fa989822345ca2c7ce4f14a33aca8ead355d6 (tree 28ce6857d84a17b1cac2bd3e2fa2eb7dd1b9d07f) for request 0052053d, under builder promise 426f2a34. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nI read the note a50ebd01 first, then the planner's amendment ecefed5c in full, then every source and test change from Main 71636079.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- Base and delta. The head descends from Main 71636079. Its four commits change exactly the 31 paths named by the 31 artifacts. The gate checkpoint 97073766 has tree f2483f95, as the note says. From the gate to the head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- Evidence. The committed manifest has SHA-256 92efbf05…, as the note says. All 194 files in the sealed directory match it. No entry is writable. SHA256SUMS has 193 lines, all OK.\n- Gate. ordinary-gate-repaired/result.json records exit 0 at 97073766, tree f2483f95, with clean status before and after and no signals. The failed first gate at 340af957 and the failed follow-ups are kept separately, and the note does not claim that the later pass explains the earlier pending resolver.\n- Numeric controls. Both JSON results bind 20 source files. I compared each file's hash with its blob at the gate commit: 0 mismatches in each set. The components add up: 1,835,008 + 104,448 + 12,715 + 942,368 = 2,894,539, and 2,726,387 + 1,467,917 = 4,194,304. The synthetic inputs B 1,310,720, Q 151,197 and P 6,000 add up to 1,467,917. Both results label themselves as not historical W1 and record the missing received lengths as unavailable.\n- Documents. check-doc-links on an export of the head: 713 links, 0 broken. check-notes 71636079..330fa989: 0 warnings. check-commit-messages: 4 checked, 0 flagged.\n- I did not rerun any suite. The gate passed at the same source.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe design is sound and narrow. Reservations belong to the budget that issued them. `ArchiveBudget.reserve` admits the whole ceiling through the existing `spend`, so pre-read admission is unchanged. It returns a frozen, empty object, which is used as a key in a private WeakMap. `settle` replaces only the metadata ceiling with the received bytes. It scales input work by the same ratio, rounding up. Calls, nodes, entries and attempts stay charged. A zero-metadata reservation releases no input work.\n\nThe Git package gains only the `JournalReservationBudget` port, which only frontier capture and validation need. For the frontier, `work.check()` followed by `reserve({metadata, inputWork})` gives the same gross charge as the old `work.metadata(maximum)`.\n\nThe converted sites match the scope in ecefed5c:\n- target and source births (including the grow driver in apps/room/archive.ts);\n- the two item queries;\n- every pin SQL call site (driver, build, inventory, prefix and window);\n- frontier ownership;\n- the two migration POST collectors.\n\nInput-only tariffs, GET fees and fixed fees are left as they were.\n\n`archivePinReadBytes` accepts only the three row shapes of the three pin SELECTs. I checked these against `readPin`: `k,bytes,type`, `v` and `hash`. Its largest possible result equals the 17,408 ceiling, so valid rows can never trigger `invalid-received`. A future change to a pin SELECT that adds a column would stop the pass rather than over-credit it. That is the safe way to fail.\n\n## Security\n\nEvery converted read is admitted at its full ceiling before I/O. Partial credit is never given before a read.\n\n`settle` calls `check()` first. A stopped pass, the original deadline and the clock check therefore all apply before anything is released. The test \"late receipts and a backwards clock\" covers this, and the W2 test shows that a throwing SQL read keeps its full charge and blocks the next read.\n\nHandles that are unknown, foreign or used twice stop the pass with `unknown-reservation`. A negative, non-integer or oversized received size stops it with `invalid-received`. Neither gives credit (W3 tests).\n\nBirths settle only after `readNativeBirth` succeeds. Item queries now also refuse a reply whose UTF-8 length exceeds the payload bound, which is stricter than before. The frontier settles only after the existing reader's type, size, hash and deadline checks. The migration collectors settle only after `currentParent` or the blob check and the caller fence `check(allowed)`. `receivedObjectBytes` counts only completed POST bodies, after `#timely()`. A shared cached head promise settles once.\n\nThe diagnostic `reservationCosts` is called through `ArchiveBudget.prototype` inside the existing try/catch. It returns copied scalars only. The observer's settlement bookkeeping runs under `safely()`, and a malformed reduction only sets flags. It cannot change admission or the outcome. The new `depth \u003e 0` guard on the observed `spend` affects only the `spend` call nested inside `reserve`, so a reservation is not recorded twice.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe source change is small: about 250 lines across 15 files. Most of the new code is the observer's net-attribution bookkeeping, which follows the existing `record` pattern. I see nothing to remove.\n\n## Observation (not blocking)\n\nIn `finishRootFixedArchivePass` (archive-build.ts, at the second `readArchivePinAfterReceipt`), `readPin` catches every error and returns null. A budget stop raised inside it is therefore reported as `mismatch`. The driver then schedules a retry with reason `mismatch`, not `limit`. This was already true at Main for spend refusals at the limit. With this head it can also happen when a settlement refusal occurs, which needs a malformed SQL adapter. The other pin call sites run `budget.check()` after the read. That is cheap and would restore the correct reason here too. It changes no admission and is safe to leave for a later head.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved. Admission before I/O is complete. Failed, outstanding and late reads keep their full charge. Settlement runs only after the original fences pass. The observer has no authority.\n- Simplification: approved.\n\nThis approval does not cover any live fitting total. As (d) of ecefed5c says, that waits for the planner's capture after deployment. The landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a50ebd0128ecbf387d35b0b243efd6f0fba8784e","binding":"assigned","head":"330fa989822345ca2c7ce4f14a33aca8ead355d6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e58492f7fb1268475ca244eee91fdaa9df58d8d7\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:426f2a348c82e4f2213ef57fc5b175ce42115e24\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e58492f7fb1268475ca244eee91fdaa9df58d8d7","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}